    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import sklearn.linear_model as linear_model\n",
    "from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n",
    "from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "6937d92f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Note**. Some of the functions imported above will be useful, and in a few cases explicitly required, in the exercises below."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "9ab02ca3",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 1 Polynomial Regression with SGD\n",
    "\n",
    "In this exercise we revisit overfitting and underfitting using polynomial regression.\n",
    "Instead of solving the linear least-squares problem analytically, as in Exercise 2, we will minimize the loss with stochastic gradient descent (SGD).\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "02ab84d0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The following function generates data from a polynomial of degree $d$ with coefficients $\\{c_k\\}_{k=0}^{d}$ and additive Gaussian noise of standard deviation $\\xi$:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "y_i = \\sum_{k=0}^{d} c_k x_i^k + \\xi\\,\\mathcal N(0,1),\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where the inputs $x_i$ are sampled uniformly from a bounded interval.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 12,
   "id": "29cb8f23",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def compute_poly(coef, X):\n",
    "    return np.sum(coef*np.tile(X, (coef.size, 1)).T**np.arange(polynomial.size), axis=1).T\n",
    "\n",
    "# This function generates data from a polynomial, possibly with noise\n",
    "def generate_polynomial_data(polynomial, noise_std, n, bound, seed):\n",
    "    \"\"\"\n",
    "    Arguments:\n",
    "        polynomial : coefficients, with polynomial[k] multiplying x**k\n",
    "        noise_std  : standard deviation of the Gaussian noise\n",
    "        n          : number of data points\n",
    "        bound      : sample x uniformly from [-bound, bound]\n",
    "        seed       : random seed\n",
    "    Returns:\n",
    "        X, y       : n inputs and corresponding noisy targets\n",
    "    \"\"\"\n",
    "\n",
    "    rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n",
    "    X = np.linspace(-bound, bound, n)\n",
    "    return X,  compute_poly(polynomial, X)+ noise_std*rng.normal(size=n)\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "fa4f248c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1.1 Generate data\n",
    "\n",
    "Generate $n=20$ noisy training points from\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "y = x^2 + 0.5x^3\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "on the interval $[-3,3]$. We use a small dataset so that the effect of model complexity on overfitting is visible.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 22,
   "id": "e91648ec",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Create 20 noisy data points in the training interval [-3, 3]\n",
    "n = 20\n",
    "train_bound = 3.0\n",
    "noise_std = 1.0\n",
    "# noise_std = 0\n",
    "polynomial = np.array([0.0, 0.0, 1.0, 0.5])\n",
    "\n",
    "X_train, y_train = generate_polynomial_data(polynomial, noise_std, n, train_bound, 69)\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 26,
   "id": "1f3413d2",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
